fessional teams have by posting  photos that was you know heartening you  got professional teams like the rangers  and the devils and of course  non-professional teams like the  islanders but the point is it was nice  to see people acknowledge this  yeah and alicia you look at the photos  of this kid so much life his whole life  stretching out in front of him and then  it ends in an instant and it really is  a freak accident right there's no  avoiding this this is a fast sport  this is a contact sport  this is unbelievably unlikely to occur  and i understand the the instinct for  some people to say well maybe we need to  tighten up some of the rules or maybe  this isn't safe there are thousands of  hockey games played every single day  this type of thing is making news  because it's so rare you're also a  parent how do you grapple with some of  these  emotions and some of these  considerations you know you said exactly  what i say to my kids when i'm trying to  explain something that is so incredibly  tragic and it just defies the  imagination you can't really even  imagine that it would happen is that i i  would i tell mine because i have  teenagers at home i'm a parent to three  tenth graders the same  grade as as teddy and what i tell them  is this is on the news because it is so  rare this is not something that happens  all the time  that does not help in any way the  heartbreak that this family and this  community is experiencing from a broader  perspective it is incredibly rare and  it's so so hard sports does come with  inherent risks  that's just the nature of sports and  every now and then every now and then  our hearts break  yeah and i'm a big hockey fan hockey  players are known to be a very  close-knit community which is why you're  seeing this outpouring including from  some of these professional franchises  and lisa jimmy touch on this a little  bit  of course our hearts go out to teddy's  family i mean you just truly cannot  imagine there also i think has to be  some rallying around whoever the kid was  who had this accident who caused it it's  not his fault this was never anyone's  intent but my goodness that will be with  that individual that young man for the  rest of his life too  now that's such a good point and look  they're 10th graders so these you guys  are are babies and teddy's dad was in  attendance so praying for him and his  family as well  i can't even imagine that and look  anytime we have tragedy the natural  response is well what could we have done  to prevent it what can we do in the  future and unfortunately with freak  accidents like this sometimes there's  nothing we can do and that's almost the  hardest conclusion to come to you know  my understanding is even wearing you  know a neck guard doesn't prevent from  lacerations and as was previously  pointed out you know sports there's  inherent risk i played field hockey i've  been hit in the head with field hockey  balls i tore my acl playing lacrosse but  then alternatively i've slipped in the  shower and gotten a concussion so life  is full of risk sports are full of risk  and and sadly i don't think there was  much that could have been done to  prevent this but as we should all pray  for his family pray for the young man  who you know was involved in this and as  you pointed out i just cannot imagine  having to go through that in life the  10th grade is so young so just praying  for everyone involved in this it's it's  really sad  yeah  absolutely and we got to leave it there
 

